  <h3 id="result-details">Result Details</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sediment Reduction:</strong> Decreased from <strong>0.42 wt%</strong> to <strong>0.06 wt%</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Water Removal:</strong> Reduced from <strong>38 vol%</strong> to <strong>1.2 vol%</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Metal Content:</strong> Combined Aluminum and Silicon decreased from <strong>881 ppm</strong> to <strong>222 ppm</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2020-09/documents/1.11_waste_oil_combustion.pdf">Ash Content:</a></strong> Reduced from <strong>1.17 wt%</strong> to <strong>0.09 wt%</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Enhanced Recovery:</strong> Increased recovery rate by approximately <strong>20%</strong> due to effective rag layer separation.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Operating Economics -->
  <h2 id="operating-economics">Operating Economics</h2>
  <p><em>The figures below are our own measured results from this specific installation - not an independently audited study.</em></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Heat-and-settle is energy- and labor-intensive: continuous tank heating, frequent sludge removal from settling tanks, and recoverable oil left behind in the tank bottoms.</li>
    <li>The self-cleaning DMPX-028 runs continuously, so electricity is the only significant operating input - roughly $0.0009 per gallon (about 0.09 cents per gallon at 15 cents/kWh), about $4,500 per year at this facility's ~4.8 million gallon annual volume.</li>
    <li>The centrifuge recovered approximately 20% more sellable oil than heat-and-settle by breaking the rag-layer emulsion that settling leaves behind.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The net effect was a large reduction in annual operating cost plus a meaningful gain in recovered-oil revenue. Recovered used oil is sold to re-refiners or burned as on-spec fuel; keeping it clean and dewatered also avoids the far higher disposal cost of contaminated oil (contaminated used oil can cost up to roughly 10x clean oil to dispose of).</p>

  <!-- Environmental Advantages -->
